NOTES
  * access/fr = READ+WRITE commands per video frame. ACT/fr = ACTIVE
    commands per frame; rowmiss = ACT/access. Bank 0 sets
    JTFRAME_BA0_AUTOPRECH so its row closes on every access and its
    rowmiss is 100% BY CONSTRUCTION; banks 1-3 keep the row open, so
    THEIR rowmiss is the real row-thrash number.
  * 'reopen' is the reporter's own same-row statistic, ACTIVE-to-ACTIVE:
    a row closed and reopened UNCHANGED (refresh precharges all banks).
    It is churn, not the hit rate. 'lngst' is the CUMULATIVE longest
    such run at the END of the phase; '+' means it grew inside it.
  * kiB/s is USEFUL data (4 words per access on BA0/2/3, 2 on BA1).
    data-bus % charges every access the full 4-word mode-register
    burst; it is still a FLOOR — turnaround and refresh are not counted.
  * The ROM download is WRITES, one command per byte, and it is the
    only phase where ACTIVE tracks traffic (the prog path passes
    match=0, jtframe_sdram64.v:331, so every byte re-activates).
    Its kiB/s and data-bus figures are therefore INFLATED — they charge
    each single-byte write a full 4-word burst. Read the download row
    as a command-rate baseline, not as bandwidth.

########################################################################
# THE VERDICT — can bank 1 absorb the tenant object traffic?
# GO on memory-system grounds. It BOUNDS THE HEADROOM; it does not prove
# the repacked design, and the difference is stated below.
########################################################################

THE STRUCTURAL POINT FIRST. A bank repack moves WHICH bank serves a fetch.
It creates no fetch and destroys none, so TOTAL BUS LOAD IS INVARIANT BY
CONSTRUCTION: 18.2% of the 96 MHz x 16-bit bus in-match, before and after.
Bandwidth was never the question; the question is whether ONE bank can carry
PCM and object traffic together without row-thrashing, and whether it can do
so inside the per-scanline deadline.

1. THE ROW-THRASH RISK IN BANK 1 IS EMPIRICALLY VOID, because the PCM stream
   HAS NO LOCALITY TO LOSE. Measured in-match: bank 1 runs at 14,132
   accesses/frame and 13,964 ACTIVEs — a 98.8% ROW MISS RATE (99.0% on the
   select screen; 78.6% in attract, where fewer channels are active). QSound
   round-robins 16 channels at unrelated addresses, so almost every fetch
   already opens a new row. Interleaving object fetches into that stream
   cannot make bank 1's PCM behaviour worse than it already is.

2. WHAT THE REPACK WOULD COST IS THE OBJECT STREAM'S OWN LOCALITY. Object and
   scroll traffic in bank 3 runs at only 28.8% row miss in-match (36.8% on
   select) — tile fetches come in runs. Moved into bank 1 beside a stream
   that changes row on nearly every access, that would degrade toward 100%.
   PRICED: every GFX access that loses its row hit costs STW-HIT = 6 extra
   SDRAM clocks. Charging ALL of bank 2 + bank 3 that penalty is 78,120
   clocks = 4.9% of a frame.

3. THE WORST-CASE BOUND, and it is generous on purpose. Assume BOTH fighters
   are tenants AND that the whole of banks 2+3 redirects — scroll included,
   which it would not be — AND that every access in bank 1 is a row miss:

       bank 1 after  =  PCM 14,132 + bank2 1,017 + bank3 17,467
                     =  32,616 accesses/frame, all misses
                     =  424,008 of 1,609,728 SDRAM clocks = 26.3% of a frame
       single-bank all-miss ceiling (STW = 13 clocks at 96 MHz)
                     =  123,825 transactions/frame
       => the worst case uses 26.3% of one bank's capacity.

   THE EXISTENCE PROOF: bank 0 ALREADY RUNS HARDER THAN THAT TODAY, in stock
   shipping configuration. It carries 40,797 accesses/frame at a 100% row
   miss rate (JTFRAME_BA0_AUTOPRECH=1 closes its row on every access) =
   32.9% of a frame — 1.25x the repack's worst case for bank 1.

4. THE DEADLINE. Per scanline the core has 6,144 SDRAM clocks (512 pixel
   clocks at 8 MHz). Worst-case bank 1 = 32,616/262 = 124.5 transactions x 13
   clocks = 1,618 of 6,144 = 26% of the line.

5. CONTENTION SIGNAL: `WARNING: (test.cpp) SDRAM reads clashed` fired ZERO
   times in 2,800 frames (the harness prints up to 25).

TWO NAMED WATCH ITEMS FOR THE RTL BRING-UP:
  a. PRIORITY INVERSION. `jtframe_sdram64.v:536-542` with BAPRIO=1 grants
     strictly bank0 > bank1 > bank2 > bank3. Moving object fetches into
     bank 1 RAISES their priority above the scroll left behind in bank 3.
     At 26% worst-case occupancy nothing starves, but this is a scheduling
     change, not just a placement change.
  b. bank 2 is nearly idle today (1,017 accesses/frame, 1.4% share) while
     bank 3 carries 23.8%. vsav's object art sits overwhelmingly in the
     `rom0_bank[0]=1` half. If the repack ever needs a second home, bank 2
     is the empty one — but its 16 MB is vanilla GFX and moving it would
     break the superset invariant, so this is an observation, not a plan.

THE ASSUMPTION, STATED PLAINLY. Only STOCK traffic is measurable: a WIDE
romset does not load on the stock core (the art does not fit, and the
promoted tile-code bit does not exist in stock RTL). So:
  * WHAT IS PROVEN: the traffic the repack would relocate, its locality, and
    that a single jtframe SDRAM bank on this very core already sustains more
    than the relocation's worst case.
  * WHAT IS NOT PROVEN: the repacked design itself. Un-measured are the
    arbitration behaviour once bank 1 carries two streams, and whether
    tenant art changes the fetch COUNT. On the latter the structure helps:
    at most two fighters are on screen either way, so a 21-character roster
    does not raise the per-match sprite count, and the promoted tile-code
    bit adds address bits, not fetches.
  * WHAT WOULD PROVE IT: the same instrument re-run on a cps2w core carrying
    the repacked map and a wide romset — i.e. this measurement is the go/no-go
    signal the ruling asked for, and the gate for the repack itself.

ONE MORE MEASURED FACT THE REPACK ARC NEEDS: bank 1's UPPER 8 MB IS EMPTY.
The post-download image measures 0.0% non-zero above 8 MB in banks 0 and 1,
against 87.6% / 90.1% in banks 2 and 3 (build/sdram_model_fix_14z107.log).
The space the ruling wants is there and is untouched.

